For:

N = 5

Output:

1
10
11
100
101

Queue-based idea:

Start:
"1"

Take "1"
Generate:
"10"
"11"

Take "10"
Generate:
"100"
"101"

This tests whether you understand how a queue can generate data level-by-level.

19. How can a Queue be used to find the first non-repeating character in a stream?

Suppose characters arrive:

a a b c

You need to track which character is currently the first non-repeating one.

Typical tools:

Queue + frequency array/map

This tests Queue + Hashing together.

20. How can Queue be used to implement BFS?

This is one of the most important questions.

For a graph:

        1
       / \
      2   3
     / \
    4   5

BFS visits:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4 → 5

We use:

queue<int> q;

because nodes are processed in the order they were discovered.

This becomes extremely important when we reach Trees and Graphs.

🧩 LEVEL 5 — Advanced Data Structure Questions
21. What is a Monotonic Queue?

A Monotonic Queue maintains elements in increasing or decreasing order.

For example:

Decreasing:

[9, 7, 5, 3]

It is commonly implemented using a deque.

One of its most important applications is:

Sliding Window Maximum

LeetCode:

239 — Sliding Window Maximum

This is an important medium/hard DSA pattern.

22. Why do we use a Deque instead of a normal Queue for Sliding Window Maximum?

Because we need to:

remove elements from the front
remove smaller elements from the rear
insert new elements at the rear

A normal Queue doesn't allow efficient operations at both ends.

A Deque does.

23. What is the difference between Queue and Priority Queue?

Normal Queue:

FIFO

Priority Queue:

Highest/lowest priority comes first

Example:

Elements:
5, 1, 10, 3

A max-priority queue gives:

10 → 5 → 3 → 1

It does not follow normal FIFO.

24. What data structure is generally used to implement a Priority Queue efficiently?

A:

Heap

Usually:

Binary Heap

In C++:

priority_queue<int> pq;
25. What is the complexity of operations in a Priority Queue?

For a binary heap:

Operation	Complexity
Insert	O(log n)
Delete top	O(log n)
Top	O(1)
Build heap	O(n)
🧠 LEVEL 6 — Tricky Interview Questions
26. Can Queue have multiple front elements?

No.

A queue has one logical front and one logical rear.

27. Is Queue always implemented using an array?

No.

It can be implemented using:

Array
Linked List
Two Stacks
Deque

and other specialized structures.

28. Why is q.pop() in C++ not returning the removed element?

This is a classic C++ STL question.

In:

q.pop();

the element is removed but not returned.

If you need it:

int x = q.front();
q.pop();

This is because the STL separates accessing and removing the element.

29. What happens if you call front() on an empty STL queue?

This is invalid behavior; you should check:

if (!q.empty()) {
    cout << q.front();
}

before accessing it.

30. Why is std::queue called a container adaptor?

Because std::queue isn't a completely independent container implementation.

It provides a queue interface over an underlying container such as:

deque

By default, C++ std::queue uses std::deque underneath.

